# Pages that are deliberately over the 8 KB ceiling, and why.
#
# The ceiling exists because every byte of a page is paid by the agent that opens
# it. A page is normally split when it exceeds the ceiling. These two are not,
# because splitting them would cost the reader more than the bytes do: each is
# opened to look one thing up, and the answer would land on the other side of the
# split as often as not.
#
# An exception is not free. A page listed here is reported on every run, so the
# pressure to shrink it stays visible. An entry naming a page that does not exist,
# or one that is already under the ceiling, is a defect and is reported.
#
# There is a second, hard ceiling of 20 KB, and nothing here raises it. A size above
# it cannot be recorded in this file, and a page above it fails whatever this file
# says: it is split, or what a tool can answer moves into the schema. It exists
# because every raise argued here has been granted, which left the six largest pages
# as the six exempt ones and turned the ceiling into a report rather than a limit.
#
#   <page> = <bytes> # reason
#
01-runbook.md       = 16128  # the whole procedure for building an application, scaffold to passing scenario, and the page AGENTS.md sends every such task to first. Not split: it is read whole in a run's first request, so a split only adds a request and an ordering to get wrong. Raised 15616 -> 15872 for the command timeout beside the one call that needs it: build_project.py's first call compiles the framework, and the warning was printed by that call, after the timeout for it had already been chosen. AGENTS.md carried the sentence, this page issues the command, and the golden path says not to compose from anywhere else. It lived under examples/ai-benchmark/ until the benchmark was found to measure a procedure a user's own agent never received: examples/ is optional and not installed, and AGENTS.md taught an older chain. Nothing in it is benchmark-specific; the benchmark's own rules are in its prompt.  Raised 15872 -> 16128 on 2026-09-17 when the worksheet became the one place a body is edited: section 6 now says a body changed after a build or a scenario run is changed in bodies.txt, that a failing check names the section it is in, and that --regenerate restores every body, and the paragraph that sent the reader to hand-edit a generated file came out. Net 246 bytes. The hunt it replaces was 15 requests and 18% of run 20260917c; the bytes are worth about $0.002 a run. reviewed 2026-09-17
22-state-machine.md = 18560  # the whole .fsml format, and it cannot be half-learned: History, OnFinal, Submachine, the guard grammar and the transition rules answer each other. The only other place any of them is written down is a 50 KB schema that gives the grammar and not the meaning, and a run that had to go there carried 13,000 tokens of it for sixty turns. Raised once more for the two facts a pause/resume design cannot be built without and neither the page nor the schema stated: that a restored state re-runs its EntryList, and that Source="Value" is a verbatim C++ token. Both were inferred from a recipe comment in a measured run; 685 bytes here is the cheapest they can be bought. Raised again for the queued OnFinal self-event: an operation on a nested Final runs before the composite is left, so the next request is refused as busy. Nothing in the page, the schema or the generated code said so, and finding it took 22 of 81 requests and 27% of one measured run. check_contract.py rule 108 now reports the placement, but a rule that fires on a page that does not explain it only moves the cost. Raised once more for the table of top-level declaration lists: the page used StimulusKind="Event" and EVENT_ throughout and never showed the <EventList> that declares the name, so a measured run reached for Method MethodType="Event", was refused as error[45], and spent four requests reading the 50 KB schema to find out where an Event goes. Raised again for the two facts that cost each of two runs measured at c926c1aa a cmake configure and a rebuild: that state names are unique across the whole document and not per level, which the page never said while its own example named the top-level marker Start, so both runs named a nested marker Start and were refused as error[3]; and for the state names being unique document-wide. Lowered 21504 -> 18432 when the grammar and the document-shaped semantics moved into fsml.xsd, where tools/schema_help.py answers them per query: the History marker rules, what a restored state does on entry, the queued OnFinal ordering, the Submachine document rules, the guard node grammar and Source="Value". What stays is what the schema cannot carry -- the generated C++ API, the worked example, and the routing to the tool. Raised 18432 -> 18560 to route the document itself at tools/agent/gen_docs.py: the ID numbering, the To resolution and the Layout advice came out, because the tool now does all three from a JSON description. The XML worked example, the pieces table and the History/OnFinal/Submachine/Guard fragments were then rewritten in that same JSON, which is what the page's authors now write; the page fell to 17.4 KB and the exception is kept for the headroom the next feature needs. reviewed 2026-09-14
51-debug.md         = 14848  # a symptom index; a split makes the reader guess which half their symptom is in. "Running the pieces" already came out of it as 50-running.md. Raised once when the generator began printing rule numbers: the refusal section now shows the message shape it must be read against, which is what stops the reader guessing at a finding instead of quoting it. Raised by one row for a broadcast that fired once before the subscription request reached the provider: the page listed every other cause of "an attribute or broadcast never arrives" and not that one, which is the one that leaves the application hanging with subscription code that reads as correct. Two of the thirteen recipes shipped it. Raised 13568 -> 14848 for "An attribute update that is never sent": a measured run spent $0.77 and three wrong diagnoses on a state the service published no value for, behind the default OnChange, and this page -- the one opened once something has already gone wrong -- carried no OnChange row at all while 20-service-interface.md already predicted the exact symptom. The rule belongs where the rule is taught; the symptom belongs here reviewed 2026-09-14
50-running.md       = 11264  # the page that turns "it built" into "it is proven to work". Raised when scenarios gained stdin, stop and reject: nearly every task asks a long-running multi-process application to prove a console quit path and a peer-loss path, and before those keys existed an agent hand-rolled mtrouter, sleep and pkill to check them -- 28 turns of one measured run. The console-loop shape here is the other half: a loop that treats end of input as a quit request shuts the service down in milliseconds with no error to read, and cost 55 turns of the next run. Showing the correct shape beside the scenario key is the only place it is read in time. 51-debug.md raised by one row for the same queued-OnFinal symptom, and by four lines pointing step 1 at run_scenarios.py --verbose: an agent that cannot get the raw output of a run from the runner starts the processes by hand, which section 9 of the runbook forbids and which cost 28 turns of an earlier run. Raised for the console-mode mechanism, which the page had wrong: it said a console mtrouter never binds 8181, and measurement says it binds and routes normally and then quits at end of input, so an agent reading the old text hunted a binding failure that was not there. The corrected text also carries what no page carried: that no option at all means --console, that --help prints the whole option table, and which processes a project needs from the --mode it was set up with. reviewed 2026-09-14
42-runtime-api.md   = 10240  # the lookup page for the runtime types, and section 8 is the only place in the corpus that names a synchronization primitive. The 2026-09-14 audit found no mutex, lock or synchronization event anywhere in docs/agent/, while 37-threads.md and recipes/07-worker-events/ teach the one shape that needs one -- a worker thread sharing state with a component -- and the only route offered was examples/, which AGENTS.md says may be absent. Eight signatures with verified Header.hpp:line citations, and the row that matters most: a component owns its state on its own thread and needs no lock at all. Not split: it is opened to look one call up, and a split doubles the guess about which half holds it. reviewed 2026-09-14
31-consumer.md      = 8704   # 35 bytes under the ceiling before the 2026-09-14 audit, so the first correctness edit tripped it. What it gained: the first line saying what the page is for, which the audit found undeclared for all of 30/31/32, and the clause that a scaffolded project ends with quit_with(code) -- without it this page prescribes the call check_contract.py reports as P-18. Splitting a page this size to hold 200 bytes of correction costs the reader a request. reviewed 2026-09-14
entry-path          = 35994  # not a page: the sum of everything a run opens before it has done anything -- AGENTS.md 11548, docs/agent/01-runbook.md 15871, and the project's own AGENTS.md 8413 that setup_project.py writes. Measured at 46 708 B with the prompt in run 20260915b: 8.9% of that bill, paid by every run and read in its first two requests. Each of the three is inside its own limit, which is exactly why this is needed: three pages a little under their caps is a bigger entry path than one page over. AGENTS.md itself is not under docs/agent/ and has no page budget at all, so this is the only thing that caps it. Set at the measured sum with no slack: raising it is a decision about a recurring cost every run pays, and it should cost an argument. Raised 35832 -> 35994 on 2026-09-17, net of the rework in the entry itself above: section 6 of the runbook grew and the platform note, the -j rule duplicated from AGENTS.md and the section that re-described section 5 came out. It is not the largest recurring cost a run has: measured across five runs on 2026-09-17, every page a run opens is 10.6% to 12.4% of its bill and the entry path alone is 4.2%, while the fix loop between the first scenario run and the last was 4 requests in the cheapest run and 44 in the dearest. See .claude/analyze/20260917-cost-regression-audit-v1.md. reviewed 2026-09-17
